    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Houses on posts and opening a new kampung",
    text: "Most island houses were raised on posts of hardwood or bamboo, with walls of plaited bamboo or palm leaf and thatch of nipa or sago palm, cool, flood-safe and quickly repaired. Thatch needed renewing every few years and posts replacing as termites ate them; neighbours came to help, fed by the household. Building began on a lucky day, with offerings buried under the main post. Founding a new settlement, to buka kampung in Malay or open a new desa in Java, meant clearing land with the ruler's leave, planting fruit trees to claim it, and establishing a shrine or later a prayer house before the first harvest.",
