    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Chinese coins, debts and bondservants in Majapahit",
    text: "By the thirteenth century Chinese copper cash had become Java's everyday money, imported by the shipload, and Majapahit's markets and taxes ran on it; excavations at Trowulan have found hoards of coins and terracotta piggy banks. Loans in coin or rice carried interest, and Old Javanese law books had rules on debts, pledges and the limits of what a lender could claim. A family that could not pay might give a member as a hulun, a bondservant, until the debt was worked off. A household recovering from a failed harvest leaned first on kin and the village, then on a lord's protection.",
    wiki: ["Cash coins in Indonesia", "Trowulan", "Debt bondage"],
