    evidence: "documented",
    title: "The blessing of the elders and the widow's half",
    text: "Filipino households were bilateral and close, and elders were honoured: children and grandchildren greeted them by pressing the elder's hand to their forehead, the mano, asking a blessing, and old parents lived with a married child, cared for at home. Spanish law gave a widow half the conjugal property outright, plus a share of her husband's half beside the children, and widows commonly ran farms, stores and trading ventures in their own names; in Manila many of the busiest market vendors and moneylenders were women. Mourning meant black clothes, novenas for the dead, and a year without fiestas.",
    wiki: ["Mano (gesture)", "Filipino values", "Utang na loob"],
