    text: "Māori religion ran through every task. The atua, departmental gods, each held a realm: Tāne the forest and birds, Tangaroa the sea, Rongo cultivated food, Tūmatauenga war, Haumia wild plants. People did not gather to worship in temples; they made offerings at a tūāhu, a simple shrine of stones or sticks, and through tohunga who recited karakia. The first fish, bird or kūmara of the season went to the god. Tapu, the state of being set apart, surrounded chiefs, the dead, and sacred work, and had to be lifted with water and cooked food before ordinary life resumed. Family ancestors watched over their descendants.",
