    text: "Rights to Māori land came from ancestry, conquest, gift and, above all, ahi kā, keeping fires burning: continuous occupation. A family that left land too long could lose its claim. Moving somewhere new meant joining kin who held land there, being given land by a chief, or taking it by force. Marriages were often arranged between hapū to make alliances and settle peace, and a couple might live with either side's people; children held rights through both parents' lines. Building a future together meant raising children who knew both whakapapa and keeping the fires alight on the lands they came from.",
