    title: "Rongoā: kawakawa for an aching tooth",
    text: "Māori plant medicine, rongoā, drew on the native bush. For toothache, leaves of kawakawa, a pepper tree with a numbing, peppery taste, were chewed and held against the sore tooth or gum; kawakawa was also used for cuts and stomach aches. Koromiko leaf treated diarrhoea, harakeke gum was put on wounds and burns, and mānuka bark and leaves eased fever. Illness with no obvious cause was thought to come from breaching tapu or from mākutu, sorcery, and was the business of a tohunga, who used karakia and water to restore balance. Teeth wore quickly from gritty fern root and shellfish sand.",
